1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:00,798
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,848 --> 00:00:06,461
In this lecture we secure the two Azure platform services almost every workload depends on:

3
00:00:06,511 --> 00:00:12,125
Key Vault, which holds secrets, keys and certificates, and Storage, which holds blobs, data

4
00:00:12,175 --> 00:00:13,805
lake files and file shares.

5
00:00:13,855 --> 00:00:19,220
We'll put both behind a Palo Alto Networks VM-Series firewall in a hub virtual network.

6
00:00:20,170 --> 00:00:24,988
This builds on the general lecture about securing PaaS behind a Palo Alto firewall.

7
00:00:25,038 --> 00:00:28,331
Here we go deep on what's specific to these two services:

8
00:00:28,381 --> 00:00:33,844
their DNS zones, their sub-resources, their firewall exceptions, the Palo Alto policy, and how

9
00:00:33,894 --> 00:00:36,600
to stop data leaving to someone else's account.

10
00:00:37,550 --> 00:00:39,973
Here's the reference topology.

11
00:00:40,023 --> 00:00:47,720
The hub VNet, 10.0.0.0/16, holds two VM-Series firewalls behind an internal load balancer with

12
00:00:47,770 --> 00:00:49,121
an HA ports rule.

13
00:00:49,171 --> 00:00:55,220
Its frontend, 10.0.2.100, is the next hop for every route in this lecture.

14
00:00:56,170 --> 00:00:58,827
Spoke one holds the application:

15
00:00:58,877 --> 00:01:04,157
a VM at 10.1.1.10 and an App Service app with VNet integration.

16
00:01:04,207 --> 00:01:10,840
Its route table sends the private endpoint range, 10.2.0.0/16, to the firewall.

17
00:01:11,790 --> 00:01:14,120
Spoke two holds the private endpoints:

18
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:20,382
the vault at 10.2.1.6, and separate endpoints for the storage account's blob, dfs and file services.

19
00:01:20,432 --> 00:01:26,769
Network policies are enabled on that subnet, and its network security group allows only the firewalls.

20
00:01:26,819 --> 00:01:29,900
Both services have public network access disabled.

21
00:01:30,850 --> 00:01:37,293
On-premises connects over ExpressRoute or VPN, and its traffic is also steered to the firewall.

22
00:01:37,343 --> 00:01:42,898
In the DNS subnet, the DNS Private Resolver gives on-premises an address to query,

23
00:01:42,948 --> 00:01:48,640
10.0.3.4, and the privatelink zones for the vault and storage are linked to the hub.

24
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:53,354
Before any firewall rules, one concept that trips people up:

25
00:01:53,404 --> 00:01:55,261
both services have two planes.

26
00:01:55,311 --> 00:02:00,156
The control plane is Azure Resource Manager, at management dot azure dot com.

27
00:02:00,206 --> 00:02:04,861
That's where you create the vault, change its networking and assign roles.

28
00:02:04,911 --> 00:02:08,357
The vault and storage firewalls don't govern it at all.

29
00:02:08,407 --> 00:02:14,460
Protect it with Entra ID, Conditional Access, Privileged Identity Management and resource locks.

30
00:02:15,410 --> 00:02:18,609
The data plane is the service endpoint itself:

31
00:02:18,659 --> 00:02:24,189
reading a secret from kv-demo dot vault dot azure dot net, or uploading a blob.

32
00:02:24,239 --> 00:02:29,840
That's what private endpoints, the service firewalls, and our Palo Alto control.

33
00:02:30,790 --> 00:02:32,862
So think of two locks on every door.

34
00:02:32,912 --> 00:02:35,810
The network decides where a request may come from.

35
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:37,756
Identity decides who may do what.

36
00:02:37,806 --> 00:02:44,064
A private endpoint without role-based access still lets any workload behind the firewall read every secret.

37
00:02:44,114 --> 00:02:47,955
Strong roles on a public endpoint still leave the service exposed.

38
00:02:48,005 --> 00:02:48,780
You need both.

39
00:02:49,730 --> 00:02:50,856
Key Vault first.

40
00:02:50,906 --> 00:02:56,439
You create a private endpoint with the sub-resource vault, here at 10.2.1.6.

41
00:02:56,489 --> 00:03:00,480
It maps to this one vault, not to Key Vault as a whole.

42
00:03:01,430 --> 00:03:01,673
DNS:

43
00:03:01,723 --> 00:03:06,359
the application keeps using kv-demo dot vault dot azure dot net.

44
00:03:06,409 --> 00:03:12,290
Public DNS returns a CNAME to kv-demo dot privatelink dot vaultcore dot azure dot

45
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:18,660
net, and the private zone privatelink dot vaultcore dot azure dot net answers 10.2.1.6.

46
00:03:18,710 --> 00:03:21,588
Notice the zone is vaultcore, not vault.

47
00:03:21,638 --> 00:03:27,958
On-premises, forward both public zones, vault dot azure dot net and vaultcore dot azure

48
00:03:28,008 --> 00:03:31,180
dot net, to the resolver's inbound endpoint.

49
00:03:32,130 --> 00:03:35,373
On the vault, set public network access to disabled.

50
00:03:35,423 --> 00:03:36,197
One subtlety:

51
00:03:36,247 --> 00:03:42,277
the setting "Allow trusted Microsoft services to bypass this firewall" still applies when public

52
00:03:42,327 --> 00:03:43,480
access is disabled.

53
00:03:43,530 --> 00:03:48,990
Those services reach the vault over the Microsoft backbone, without a private endpoint.

54
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:54,120
Use the Azure role-based access model, give apps only Key Vault Secrets User, and

55
00:03:54,170 --> 00:04:00,200
turn on soft delete and purge protection, so a compromised admin can't permanently destroy keys.

56
00:04:01,150 --> 00:04:03,000
So who goes through your firewall?

57
00:04:03,050 --> 00:04:08,253
Your VMs, App Service with VNet integration, and on-premises do, through the private endpoint.

58
00:04:08,303 --> 00:04:14,289
Trusted services like disk encryption, SQL transparent data encryption, Azure Backup and Application Gateway

59
00:04:14,339 --> 00:04:15,910
certificate retrieval do not;

60
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:18,648
they're governed by roles and the bypass setting.

61
00:04:18,698 --> 00:04:23,287
And services not on the trusted list, like hosted Azure DevOps agents, are blocked,

62
00:04:23,337 --> 00:04:27,199
so run a self-hosted agent in a spoke and it goes through the firewall

63
00:04:27,249 --> 00:04:28,540
like any other workload.

64
00:04:29,490 --> 00:04:37,278
Storage is different, because one account exposes several services, and each needs its own private endpoint:

65
00:04:37,328 --> 00:04:41,560
blob, dfs, file, queue, table, and web for static websites.

66
00:04:42,510 --> 00:04:44,667
Each has its own private DNS zone:

67
00:04:44,717 --> 00:04:50,316
privatelink dot blob dot core dot windows dot net, privatelink dot dfs, privatelink dot

68
00:04:50,366 --> 00:04:51,355
file, and so on.

69
00:04:51,405 --> 00:04:58,497
If the account uses read-access geo-redundant storage, the secondary endpoints are separate sub-resources too,

70
00:04:58,547 --> 00:04:59,860
using the same zones.

71
00:05:00,810 --> 00:05:01,747
On the account:

72
00:05:01,797 --> 00:05:07,472
public network access disabled, shared key access off so only Entra ID identities work,

73
00:05:07,522 --> 00:05:10,564
anonymous blob access off, and minimum TLS 1.2.

74
00:05:10,614 --> 00:05:11,420
And remember:

75
00:05:11,470 --> 00:05:17,078
the storage firewall's IP and virtual network rules apply only to the public endpoint.

76
00:05:17,128 --> 00:05:22,540
Traffic through a private endpoint isn't filtered by them, which is exactly why the

77
00:05:22,590 --> 00:05:27,080
Palo Alto and the subnet's NSG are your controls on the private side.

78
00:05:28,030 --> 00:05:29,118
The classic gotcha:

79
00:05:29,168 --> 00:05:34,388
you create a blob endpoint, but your analytics tool talks to the Data Lake dfs endpoint.

80
00:05:34,438 --> 00:05:39,598
dfs still resolves to the public address, public access is off, and you get 403 errors.

81
00:05:39,648 --> 00:05:42,053
Inventory every endpoint your tools call.

82
00:05:42,103 --> 00:05:46,963
And Azure Files from on-premises needs the file endpoint, with TCP 445 open across

83
00:05:47,013 --> 00:05:48,700
the link and on the firewall.

84
00:05:49,650 --> 00:05:50,333
Now routing.

85
00:05:50,383 --> 00:05:54,483
Each private endpoint injects a slash 32 route into peered networks.

86
00:05:54,533 --> 00:05:58,878
That beats any broader route, so by default the app goes straight to the

87
00:05:58,928 --> 00:06:01,320
endpoint and the firewall never sees it.

88
00:06:02,270 --> 00:06:02,888
The fix:

89
00:06:02,938 --> 00:06:09,821
enable private endpoint network policies on the endpoint subnet, and add a route on

90
00:06:09,871 --> 00:06:16,252
every client subnet, and on the GatewaySubnet, for 10.2.0.0/16 to 10.0.2.100.

91
00:06:16,302 --> 00:06:22,684
The prefix must be at least as specific as the endpoint VNet's address space.

92
00:06:22,734 --> 00:06:26,860
A default route alone won't override the slash 32.

93
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:29,977
Then keep the return path symmetric:

94
00:06:30,027 --> 00:06:35,581
source NAT to the firewall's trust interface, or tag each firewall NIC with disableSnatOnPL

95
00:06:35,631 --> 00:06:38,291
equals true to keep the real client address.

96
00:06:38,341 --> 00:06:42,602
And close the loop with an NSG on the endpoint subnet that allows only

97
00:06:42,652 --> 00:06:48,760
the firewall's trust subnet, so anything that sneaks around the firewall is dropped at the endpoint.

98
00:06:49,710 --> 00:06:54,472
On the Palo Alto, create address objects for each endpoint and an address group

99
00:06:54,522 --> 00:06:56,056
for the storage endpoints.

100
00:06:56,106 --> 00:06:59,771
Then create a custom URL category listing exactly your names:

101
00:06:59,821 --> 00:07:03,974
kv-demo dot vault dot azure dot net, and stdemo's blob and dfs names.

102
00:07:04,024 --> 00:07:09,700
Point the firewall's DNS at the resolver so any FQDN objects resolve to the private addresses.

103
00:07:10,650 --> 00:07:15,033
Key Vault and Blob are HTTPS, so the firewall can match the TLS server

104
00:07:15,083 --> 00:07:17,946
name against that category without decrypting.

105
00:07:17,996 --> 00:07:23,962
You can decrypt if clients trust your forward-proxy certificate authority, since the Azure SDKs

106
00:07:24,012 --> 00:07:26,431
use the operating system's trust store.

107
00:07:26,481 --> 00:07:32,131
Many teams deliberately exclude the vault from decryption, because the payload is secrets.

108
00:07:32,181 --> 00:07:36,310
Azure Files over SMB 3 is encrypted end to end, so control it with

109
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:37,640
App-ID and addresses.

110
00:07:38,590 --> 00:07:39,242
The rules:

111
00:07:39,292 --> 00:07:44,717
one, the app and App Service integration subnets to the vault endpoint, SSL on

112
00:07:44,767 --> 00:07:49,631
443, restricted to our URL category, with threat profiles and logging.

113
00:07:49,681 --> 00:07:55,107
Two, the app to the blob and dfs endpoints, same pattern, adding antivirus and

114
00:07:55,157 --> 00:07:57,353
file blocking where you decrypt.

115
00:07:57,403 --> 00:08:01,565
Three, on-premises file servers to the file endpoint on 445.

116
00:08:01,615 --> 00:08:03,250
Four, the important one:

117
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:06,689
deny any other storage or vault name, and log it.

118
00:08:06,739 --> 00:08:10,269
Five, deny everything else to the endpoint subnets.

119
00:08:10,319 --> 00:08:14,060
And a NAT rule for source NAT, unless you use the tag.

120
00:08:15,010 --> 00:08:17,509
Now the traffic the firewall can't see.

121
00:08:17,559 --> 00:08:22,541
On the storage account's public side, the default action is deny, and you can

122
00:08:22,591 --> 00:08:28,880
add virtual network rules, IP rules, resource instance rules, and the trusted services exception.

123
00:08:29,830 --> 00:08:34,293
A storage account using a customer-managed key reads it from Key Vault with its

124
00:08:34,343 --> 00:08:36,864
managed identity, through the trusted bypass.

125
00:08:36,914 --> 00:08:40,977
Azure Backup, Monitor and Event Grid use the trusted services exception.

126
00:08:41,027 --> 00:08:45,490
Services like AI Search or Data Factory can use a resource instance rule, which

127
00:08:45,540 --> 00:08:49,204
allows one named resource instead of every instance of a service.

128
00:08:49,254 --> 00:08:53,260
None of these flows enter your VNets, so the Palo Alto never sees them.

129
00:08:54,210 --> 00:08:55,725
So govern them another way:

130
00:08:55,775 --> 00:09:01,059
prefer resource instance rules over the broad exception, give each managed identity only the

131
00:09:01,109 --> 00:09:04,943
data roles it needs, and turn on diagnostic logs for both services.

132
00:09:04,993 --> 00:09:10,740
The Network Security Perimeter, generally available for Key Vault and Storage, can replace scattered

133
00:09:10,790 --> 00:09:14,160
IP and instance rules with one boundary and one access log.

134
00:09:15,110 --> 00:09:16,149
Data exfiltration.

135
00:09:16,199 --> 00:09:18,023
Imagine a compromised workload.

136
00:09:18,073 --> 00:09:22,740
It can reach our storage account through the firewall, because that's allowed.

137
00:09:23,690 --> 00:09:27,336
But can it upload our data to an attacker's storage account?

138
00:09:27,386 --> 00:09:32,265
Every storage account shares the same domain, blob dot core dot windows dot net.

139
00:09:32,315 --> 00:09:35,468
If your rule allows the wildcard, the answer is yes.

140
00:09:35,518 --> 00:09:40,520
With our rule four, the firewall denies any storage or vault name that isn't ours.

141
00:09:41,470 --> 00:09:42,631
Layer the defenses:

142
00:09:42,681 --> 00:09:44,990
private endpoints map to one account;

143
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:47,348
the Palo Alto allows only your names;

144
00:09:47,398 --> 00:09:52,001
service endpoint policies cover any subnet still using service endpoints;

145
00:09:52,051 --> 00:09:56,719
disabling shared keys and SAS tokens forces Entra ID, which you can audit;

146
00:09:56,769 --> 00:10:00,798
and Defender for Storage and Key Vault alerts on unusual access.

147
00:10:00,848 --> 00:10:03,922
Review the blocked storage names in the URL logs.

148
00:10:03,972 --> 00:10:07,300
They're often the first sign of a data theft attempt.

149
00:10:08,250 --> 00:10:09,096
On-premises.

150
00:10:09,146 --> 00:10:13,277
A file server at 172.16.5.20 mounts an Azure file share.

151
00:10:13,327 --> 00:10:19,773
Traffic crosses ExpressRoute to the gateway, and a route on the GatewaySubnet sends the

152
00:10:19,823 --> 00:10:25,000
endpoint range to the firewall, which allows 445 to the file endpoint.

153
00:10:25,950 --> 00:10:27,199
DNS makes it work:

154
00:10:27,249 --> 00:10:32,902
on-premises DNS forwards file dot core dot windows dot net, vault dot azure dot

155
00:10:32,952 --> 00:10:37,523
net and vaultcore dot azure dot net to the resolver at 10.0.3.4.

156
00:10:37,573 --> 00:10:39,905
Users mount with the normal name.

157
00:10:39,955 --> 00:10:45,752
Many internet providers block port 445, but over ExpressRoute or VPN to a private

158
00:10:45,802 --> 00:10:47,918
endpoint it's private traffic.

159
00:10:47,968 --> 00:10:56,220
And use identity-based SMB authentication, Entra Kerberos or Active Directory, rather than the storage account key.

160
00:10:57,170 --> 00:11:00,389
When something fails, the error tells you where to look.

161
00:11:00,439 --> 00:11:05,702
Key Vault "client address is not authorized" means the name resolved to the public address.

162
00:11:05,752 --> 00:11:10,897
"Not an approved private link" means the endpoint is pending or belongs to another vault.

163
00:11:10,947 --> 00:11:16,151
Storage AuthorizationFailure usually means the wrong sub-resource, or the public endpoint.

164
00:11:16,201 --> 00:11:21,872
AuthorizationPermissionMismatch means the network is fine and the identity is missing a data role.

165
00:11:21,922 --> 00:11:24,499
A timeout with no firewall log means routing;

166
00:11:24,549 --> 00:11:27,360
an allowed session that ages out means asymmetry.

167
00:11:28,310 --> 00:11:29,632
Here's the checklist:

168
00:11:29,682 --> 00:11:34,010
an endpoint for every vault and every storage sub-resource you use;

169
00:11:34,060 --> 00:11:35,382
zones and forwarders;

170
00:11:35,432 --> 00:11:38,714
network policies and an NSG on the endpoint subnet;

171
00:11:38,764 --> 00:11:41,785
routes on client subnets and the GatewaySubnet;

172
00:11:41,835 --> 00:11:42,830
SNAT or the tag;

173
00:11:42,880 --> 00:11:46,163
a named-account URL category with an explicit deny;

174
00:11:46,213 --> 00:11:47,665
public access disabled;

175
00:11:47,715 --> 00:11:49,756
trusted bypass only when needed;

176
00:11:49,806 --> 00:11:53,023
Key Vault roles, soft delete and purge protection;

177
00:11:53,073 --> 00:11:55,898
storage shared key and anonymous access off;

178
00:11:55,948 --> 00:11:58,054
Azure Policy to keep it that way;

179
00:11:58,104 --> 00:12:00,014
and all the logs in one place.

180
00:12:00,064 --> 00:12:03,673
The diagrams and script are in the demo-labs repository.

181
00:12:03,723 --> 00:12:04,980
Thanks for watching.
